    stateOfArt: [
      "Metastatic first-line treatment for TNBC changed twice in 2026: sacituzumab govitecan was approved as monotherapy for patients ineligible for immunotherapy or combined with pembrolizumab for PD-L1 CPS 10 or more disease, and datopotamab deruxtecan was separately approved for patients ineligible for PD-1/PD-L1 therapy. Both are Trop-2-directed antibody-drug conjugates, marking a shift from cytotoxic chemotherapy backbones toward targeted ADCs even in a disease defined by the absence of a single dominant receptor target.",
      "Long-term follow-up of KEYNOTE-522 at a median of 75.1 months showed that neoadjuvant and adjuvant pembrolizumab reduces the risk of death by 34% compared with chemotherapy alone in high-risk early-stage TNBC, confirming that the early pathological complete response benefit translates into a durable survival advantage.",
      "Receptor status is not fixed over the course of disease: the French ESME cohort found a 27.0% total discordance rate for hormone receptor and/or HER2 status between the primary tumour and metastases, with ER status changing in 15.1% of cases and PR status in 31.1%, which is why ASCO/CAP guidance calls for retesting receptors at metastatic recurrence.",
    ],
    openProblems: [
      "TNBC lacks all three routinely druggable receptors, so chemotherapy, immunotherapy and antibody-drug conjugates remain the only broadly available systemic options, and no reliably hormone-receptor-directed adjuvant target exists outside germline BRCA carriers.",
      "The disease is molecularly and spatially heterogeneous, which undermines single-biomarker prognostic and predictive models; spatial profiling has shown sharp shifts in PAM50 subtype and immune infiltration within the same tumour.",
      "Resistance to immune checkpoint inhibition is common and appears tied to homologous-recombination status: BRCA-mutant, homologous-recombination-deficient tumours tend to have more tumour-infiltrating lymphocytes and respond better to immunotherapy, while homologous-recombination-proficient tumours are often refractory.",
      "Receptor status can change between the primary tumour and a metastasis at rates high enough to alter treatment eligibility, meaning a single primary-tumour biopsy can misclassify a patient's disease over time.",
      "No FDA-approved nuclear medicine agent identifies triple-negative lesions specifically or predicts response to TNBC-directed therapy; fluoroestradiol PET, the one approved receptor-imaging breast agent, explicitly cannot assess HER2 or progesterone receptor status and has no defined role in oestrogen-receptor-negative disease.",
    ],
